01-rosario-ponencias/gr-257761-20250422:
atty. melita go and wayne go, petitioners, vs. saint jude catholic school manila, inc., fr. marcelino nicasio, svd, fatima de guzman, and spouses eugene lee and joanne lee, respondents. iii. civil procedure > t. execution, satisfaction, and effect of judgments (rule 39) > 4. how a judgment is executed **the only costs recoverable by the prevailing party are those fixed in rule 142, sections 9 to 11; any other item claimed as costs is properly a matter of damages. reasonable attorney's fees may be recovered only as actual or compensatory damages under article 2208 of the civil code, and may not also be taxed as costs.** section 10 lists the costs recoverable in the rtc "and no other," including:
- php 15 for the complaint or answer (nothing for other pleadings or motions);
- a flat php 20 for the attendance of the party and counsel "down to and including final judgment," however many appearances there were;
- php 2 per day for each witness necessarily produced, plus lawful traveling fees;
- lawful fees paid for official copies of documents (nothing for original documents); and
- lawful docket, service and clerk's fees.

under section 6, no attorney's fees may be taxed as costs against the adverse party except as provided by civil law. a party already awarded attorney's fees as damages cannot also recover counsel's actual appearance fees as costs. in *norsk hydro*, the statement that costs are "not limited to" sections 9 to 11 refers to costs the court may tax under other rules (e.g., rule 23, sec. 18; rule 32, sec. 13), not to costs recoverable by the prevailing party. reiterating *corpus v. corpus*.

**in the rtc, a superior court, costs must first be taxed by the clerk of court.** the prevailing party gives five days' written notice with a verified statement of the items; objections are made in writing; and either party may appeal the clerk's taxation to the court (rule 142, sec. 8). taxation is a condition precedent to the obligation to pay costs, to the court's power to pass on disputed items, and to the inclusion of costs in an execution (*romulo v. dasalla*). a judge errs in ruling on the bill of costs outright instead of directing the clerk to assess it. an order granting execution is not appealable (rule 41, sec. 1), but **a ruling on the taxation of costs is final, not interlocutory, and may be appealed**. a rule 45 petition on a pure question of law regarding costs is proper (*del rosario v. bayona*). xii. damages > b. actual and compensatory damages > 4. attorney's fees and expenses of litigation

01-rosario-ponencias/am-rtj-25-084-20250:
hon. josefina b. tallado, complainant, vs. presiding judge winston s. racoma, branch 39, regional trial court, daet, camarines norte, and presiding judge arniel a. dating, branch 41, regional trial court, daet, camarines norte, respondents. ix. legal and judicial ethics > b. judicial ethics (cjca) > 2. accountability of members of the judiciary > a. judicial accountability and disciplinary proceedings (canon vii) **errors attributed to judges in the exercise of their adjudicative functions must be assailed in judicial proceedings, not in an administrative case.** examples are issuing 20-day tros without a prior summary hearing; the proper remedies are a motion for reconsideration and, if warranted, certiorari. a judge cannot be held civilly, criminally or administratively liable for official acts, however erroneous, done in good faith. **only judicial errors tainted with fraud, dishonesty, corruption, gross ignorance, bad faith, or deliberate intent to do an injustice are administratively sanctioned** (*mahinay v. judge daomilas, jr.*, reiterated).

the court applied the guidelines laid down in *tallado v. judge racoma* (2022) for determining whether a disciplinary case against a justice, judge or court personnel is a **harassment suit**:

- the existence of other cases filed against the respondent by the same or related complainants;
- the complainant's position and influence, particularly in the locality where the respondent is stationed;
- the number of times the respondent has been charged administratively, and how those cases ended;
- any decisions or judicial actions the respondent previously rendered for or against the complainant;
- the complainant's propensity for filing administrative cases against members and personnel of the judiciary; and
- any other factor indicating improper pressure or influence.

applying these guidelines to a **request for a judicial audit**, the court found the request a ploy to harass the judges. it ordered the complainant to show cause why she should not be held in **indirect contempt** for requesting a judicial audit for unfounded reasons. iv. provisional remedies > c. preliminary injunction (rule 58) > 1. preliminary injunction vs. temporary restraining order vs. status quo ante order v. special civil actions > k. contempt (rule 71) > 1. direct vs. indirect contempt vi. judicial department > e. the supreme court > 1. composition, powers, and functions > b. administrative supervision over lower courts
